<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n37585" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">prae-festīno</span></b> , āvi,
			ātum, 1, v. a.
		<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>To hasten before the time</i>, <i>to
				hasten too much</i> (rare; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">not in Cic. or Cæs.): praefestinare
					praeloqui,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plaut. Rud. 1, 2, 31</span></i>
			: sciscere, quae sit causa, Afran. ap.
				<i><span class="en">Charis. p. 186</span></i> P. (Com. Rel. p.
			182 Rib.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ne deficere praefestinarent,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Liv.
					23, 14 <i>fin.</i></span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">praefestinatum opus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Col.
					11, 2, 3</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>To hasten past</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sinum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Tac. A.
					5, 10</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
